WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.324
Welcome to Class 6: Snakemake on RCC.

2
00:00:03.324 --> 00:00:07.598
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:07.598 --> 00:00:14.246
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:14.246 --> 00:00:16.620
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:22.973
After this class, you can: explain what Snakemake controls and what Slurm controls;

6
00:00:22.973 --> 00:00:30.792
structure a workflow around inputs, outputs, rules, configuration, and logs; dry-run and inspect a workflow before

7
00:00:30.792 --> 00:00:37.634
it submits jobs; use the managed RCC IKIM profile instead of copying scheduler settings;

8
00:00:37.634 --> 00:00:45.453
request CPU, memory, time, partitions, GPUs, and local scratch per rule; keep software and data in

9
00:00:45.453 --> 00:00:53.272
the correct storage locations; resume safely after correcting a failed rule; and retain enough provenance to

10
00:00:53.272 --> 00:00:55.227
reproduce a scientific result.

11
00:00:55.227 --> 00:00:59.736
Snakemake builds a directed graph from declared input and output files.

12
00:00:59.736 --> 00:01:05.475
It decides which rules need to run and which completed outputs can be reused.

13
00:01:05.475 --> 00:01:11.214
Slurm decides where and when each submitted rule job runs and enforces its CPU,

14
00:01:11.214 --> 00:01:13.673
memory, time, partition, and GPU allocation.

15
00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:19.002
Do not run scientific rule bodies as unmanaged processes on an SSH gateway.

16
00:01:19.002 --> 00:01:25.151
Do not add sbatch calls inside a rule: the managed profile is the scheduler boundary.

17
00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:33.571
Keep durable workflow state in an approved shared project directory: Git should contain workflow logic,

18
00:01:33.571 --> 00:01:38.062
configuration templates, environment or container declarations, and documentation.

19
00:01:38.062 --> 00:01:45.360
It must not contain credentials, patient identifiers, raw research data, large generated results,

20
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.167
workflow caches, or Conda environments.

21
00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:52.617
The managed command is /usr/local/bin/snakemake, normally available as snakemake.

22
00:01:52.617 --> 00:01:59.539
Confirm the version for a retained analysis: The control environment belongs to RCC administrators.

23
00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:05.472
Analysis tools belong in reviewed per-rule Apptainer images or approved per-rule environments;

24
00:02:05.472 --> 00:02:10.416
do not install pipeline packages into the managed Snakemake environment.

25
00:02:10.416 --> 00:02:17.487
Copy the synthetic example from examples/snakemake-rcc into an approved project directory: Replace PROJECT with a project

26
00:02:17.487 --> 00:02:18.812
you may use.

27
00:02:18.812 --> 00:02:24.999
Inspect Snakefile and config.yaml, then ask Snakemake what it would do without running anything:

28
00:02:24.999 --> 00:02:31.186
Render the dependency graph when Graphviz is available: Run the bounded workflow through Slurm:

29
00:02:31.186 --> 00:02:36.047
The example creates three tiny synthetic sample files and one summary.

30
00:02:36.047 --> 00:02:41.792
It uses one CPU, 256 MiB, five minutes, and cpu_short per rule job.

31
00:02:41.792 --> 00:02:48.421
--jobs 4 is a concurrency ceiling, not a request for four CPUs in every job.

32
00:02:48.421 --> 00:02:55.593
Give each rule realistic resources based on a small measured pilot: threads becomes the rule's usable

33
00:02:55.593 --> 00:02:56.489
CPU count.

34
00:02:56.489 --> 00:02:59.627
mem_mb is host memory, not GPU VRAM.

35
00:02:59.627 --> 00:03:00.972
runtime is minutes.

36
00:03:00.972 --> 00:03:08.143
The managed profile translates the supported resource names into Slurm requests; keep cluster-specific defaults in the

37
00:03:08.143 --> 00:03:13.970
profile or project configuration rather than scattering raw scheduler flags through every rule.

38
00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:20.820
Use node-local storage only inside a Slurm rule job and only for temporary work.

39
00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:28.650
The approved pattern stages required inputs to $TMPDIR, computes there, and copies declared outputs back to

40
00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:31.096
shared project storage before success.

41
00:03:31.096 --> 00:03:36.968
Never place the Snakefile, durable results, logs, or resume-critical state under /local.

42
00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:44.797
A rule should use local scratch only when measurement justifies the staging cost—for example repeated random

43
00:03:44.797 --> 00:03:48.712
I/O, sorting, assembly, decompression, or many temporary files.

44
00:03:48.712 --> 00:03:52.137
Class 14 contains the detailed local-I/O lesson.

45
00:03:52.137 --> 00:03:56.526
Inspect the failed rule's log and its Slurm accounting record.

46
00:03:56.526 --> 00:04:03.548
Correct the input, command, environment, or resource request, then dry-run again: Snakemake reuses outputs whose declared

47
00:04:03.548 --> 00:04:05.303
dependencies are still satisfied.

48
00:04:05.303 --> 00:04:10.570
Do not delete the whole results tree as a first troubleshooting step,

49
00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:16.275
submit several copies of the same workflow, or create an automatic retry storm.

50
00:04:16.275 --> 00:04:23.297
Use explicit rerun triggers or --forcerun RULE only after understanding why the existing output must be

51
00:04:23.297 --> 00:04:23.735
replaced.

52
00:04:23.735 --> 00:04:25.930
Treat protected inputs as read-only.

53
00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:32.091
Retain: the Git commit or archived workflow version; Snakefile, included rule files,

54
00:04:32.091 --> 00:04:40.305
and resolved configuration; input inventory and checksums where appropriate; exact container digests or environment lock files;

55
00:04:40.305 --> 00:04:46.980
the Snakemake and profile versions; the resolved command and concurrency limit; rule logs,

56
00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:54.167
benchmarks, and relevant Slurm job identifiers; and output checksums and the scientific validation record.

57
00:04:54.167 --> 00:05:01.355
A technically successful workflow can still use the wrong samples, reference, parameters, or model.

58
00:05:01.355 --> 00:05:04.436
Scheduler success is not scientific validation.

59
00:05:04.436 --> 00:05:11.182
You have completed Class 6 when: the synthetic workflow dry-run lists the expected four rule jobs;

60
00:05:11.182 --> 00:05:17.928
the managed IKIM profile completes the bounded run; a second dry-run reports that nothing needs to

61
00:05:17.928 --> 00:05:23.410
be done; you can explain the difference between --jobs, threads, mem_mb, and runtime;

62
00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:29.313
you can identify which files belong in Git, shared project storage, and node-local scratch;

63
00:05:29.313 --> 00:05:34.373
and you can name the provenance required before retaining a scientific result.

